{
  "type": "instrument",
  "id": "vibraphone",
  "name": "Vibraphone (soft mallets and bowed)",
  "version": 1,
  "note": "An instrument recipe for the sampler (doc 24, Instruments, Sample preparation and Sampler rules). It describes exactly the files the prep step produced; nothing here is a tuning choice. type, id, name, version: what this is. source: where the recordings come from. channels: 1, every file is mono, taken from one microphone of the library's spaced stereo pair (see source.notes). formats: each file exists once per format under root, as <file>.ogg (Ogg Opus, 48 kHz mono, 56 kbps, variable bitrate) and <file>.m4a (AAC-LC, 48 kHz mono, 80 kbps); the browser picks the first it can play. Every file was processed at 48 kHz, so the sample positions of the decoded .ogg are the positions of the processed file (checked by cross-correlation: offset 0 for every file, length equal to the sample). The .m4a starts in the same place (offset 0) and decodes 0 to 21 ms longer, because AAC pads its last frame; for a looped file that padding lies after loop.endS and is never played. root: folder of the files, relative to the repo. layers: the most velocity layers any set has, layer 1 the quietest. sets: one entry per articulation, each with source (the library subfolder) and zones; a zone is one recorded note: note (scientific pitch, C4 = middle C, sharps written s), midi (C4 = 60). measuredCents: the zone's measured pitch offset from 12-tone equal temperament at A4 = 440 Hz, in cents, as recorded (the mean of its layers): the frequency of the first partial from a zero-padded Hann spectrum, measured in two windows (struck: 0.2 to 1 s and 1 to 2 s after the onset, 0.1 to 0.6 and 0.6 to 1.2 s above 1 kHz; looped: the second before the loop and the loop itself); tuneWindowSpreadCents is how far the two windows disagree, layerSpreadCents how far the layers disagree, and tuneConfidence (0 to 1) falls as either grows. layers: the files of the zone, quietest first; file is the path under root without extension. peakDb: sample peak in dBFS. attackRmsDb: RMS of the first 300 ms after the onset, in dBFS. brightness: share of energy above 1.5 times the fundamental (or 1 kHz, whichever is higher) in those first 300 ms. lenS: length of the file in seconds. trimStartMs: how much was cut from the start (the onset, the first point above -50 dB of the file's peak, is kept with 3 ms before it). measuredCents on a layer is that file's own measurement. src: the original file name. mic: which microphone of the pair the file was taken from. No gain was changed anywhere. levels: normalized false means the library kept the recorded levels, so the layers already step up in level as they were played. range: the lowest and highest zone. problems: anything odd found while preparing, in plain words. loop (looped sets only): startS and endS in seconds from the start of the file as encoded (sample = seconds x 48000, exact). The file ends at loop.endS. A note plays the file from 0, then repeats loop.startS to loop.endS sample for sample for as long as it is held, and the sampler's own release envelope ends it. The loop was chosen in the steady part of the sound by matching level, spectrum and level contour at both ends, the start sits on an upward zero crossing, the end was placed (on an upward crossing, within 12 ms) where the waveform best matches the material before the start, and the 250 ms before the start were blended into the last 250 ms of the loop with an equal-power crossfade corrected for that match (crossfadeCorrelation), so the wrap is seamless without any sampler crossfade. loopRmsDb: RMS of the loop region in dBFS (the held level). loopCheck, measured on the decoded .ogg with the loop played four times: seamStepVsSteadyMax, the largest sample-to-sample step at a seam divided by the largest step inside the loop (below 1 means the seam is smoother than the sound itself); seamLevelDb, the level change from the 50 ms before a seam to the 50 ms after it; naturalLevelDbMedian, the same 50 ms to 50 ms change measured everywhere inside the loop, for comparison. VIBRAPHONE: struck is soft mallets, motor off, two velocity layers, each note left to ring and cut where its 50 ms RMS falls to 60 dB below its peak or at 5 s, whichever comes first, then an equal-power fade over the last 250 ms; not looped. bowed is the bar bowed with a bass bow, one take per note, looped; the bow swells in over 1 to 2 s, so attackRmsDb says little and loopRmsDb is the level to balance by. The bowed notes were recorded at their own levels (loop RMS from -50.6 to -31.9 dBFS), so the sampler should level them by loopRmsDb, not play them as recorded.",
  "source": {
    "library": "VCSL (Versilian Community Sample Library)",
    "url": "https://github.com/sgossner/VCSL/tree/master/Idiophones/Struck%20Idiophones/Vibraphone",
    "license": "CC0 1.0",
    "commit": "c1ea7bcc3c73",
    "notes": "Folder Idiophones/Struck Idiophones/Vibraphone, 44.1 kHz 16-bit stereo, subfolders Soft Mallets (11 notes x 2 velocity layers, v1 and v2), Hard Mallets (11 notes x 2 layers, v2 and v3, not used), Bowed (6 notes, one take). There is no motor-on (vibrato) recording in the library: every soft-mallet file is motor off (the level contour of each loud layer has under 0.3 dB of periodic modulation). The file names sit one octave low: Vibes_soft_F2 sounds F3 (174.6 Hz), Vibes_bowed_D4 sounds D5 (587 Hz); every file was placed by measurement. The two microphones are spaced along the bars: per note the left-right balance runs from -13 to +17 dB and the correlation from -0.34 to 0.99, so a plain left-plus-right fold lost up to 4.2 dB and comb filtered. Each note therefore uses the one microphone that carries more of its ring (energy from 0.8 s after the onset), the same for both layers. C4 v1 and E4 v1 come from the library's rr2 takes (no rr1 exists for those). Levels are as recorded (not normalised)."
  },
